        "math_academy_id": "question-008",
        "problem": "Consider the surface ${x}^{2}{y}^{4}+{e}^{yz}{z}^{3}=4.$ Given that $n=\u27e8a,b,c\u27e9$ is a non-zero normal vector to the surface at the point $(2,-1,0),$ what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$",
        "worked_solution": "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}{y}^{4}+{e}^{yz}{z}^{3}.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=4.$  \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(2,-1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}({x}^{2}{y}^{4}+{e}^{yz}{z}^{3})i+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}({x}^{2}{y}^{4}+{e}^{yz}{z}^{3})j+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202z}({x}^{2}{y}^{4}+{e}^{yz}{z}^{3})k \\\\  & =(2x{y}^{4})i+(4{x}^{2}{y}^{3}+{z}^{4}{e}^{yz})j+({z}^{2}{e}^{yz}(yz+3))k \\\\ \u2207f(2,-1,0) & =2(2)(-1)^{4}\\text{\u00a0}i+[4(2)^{2}(-1)^{3}+{0}^{4}{e}^{(-1)(0)}]\\text{\u00a0}j+[{0}^{2}{e}^{(-1)(0)}((-1)(0)+3)]\\text{\u00a0}k \\\\  & =4\\text{\u00a0}i-16\\text{\u00a0}j+0\\text{\u00a0}k \\\\  & =\u27e84,\\,-16,\\,0\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=\u27e8a,b,c\u27e9=\u27e84,\\,-16,\\,0\u27e9.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-16}{4}=-4.$",
